## Proof

**Claim.** The necessary and sufficient condition is that the measure space has **finite total measure**, i.e., $\mu(X) < \infty$.

---

### Sufficiency ($\mu(X) < \infty \Rightarrow$ the property holds)

Assume $\mu(X) < \infty$. Let $f \in L_\infty$, so $\|f\|_\infty = \operatorname{ess\,sup}|f| < \infty$. For every $p \ge 1$:

$$|f|^p \le \|f\|_\infty^p \quad \mu\text{-a.e.},$$

so

$$\|f\|_p = \left(\int_X |f|^p\,d\mu\right)^{1/p} \le \left(\|f\|_\infty^p \cdot \mu(X)\right)^{1/p} = \|f\|_\infty \cdot \mu(X)^{1/p}.$$

Since $\mu(X) < \infty$, the function $p \mapsto \mu(X)^{1/p}$ is bounded on $[1,\infty)$: if $\mu(X) \le 1$ then $\mu(X)^{1/p} \le 1$; if $\mu(X) > 1$ then $\mu(X)^{1/p} \le \mu(X)$. In either case $\mu(X)^{1/p} \le \max(1,\mu(X))$. Therefore

$$\sup_{p\ge 1}\|f\|_p \le \|f\|_\infty \cdot \max(1,\mu(X)) < \infty.$$

Since $f \in L_\infty$ was arbitrary, the property holds. $\checkmark$

---

### Necessity (the property $\Rightarrow \mu(X) < \infty$)

We prove the contrapositive. Suppose $\mu(X) = \infty$. Consider the constant function $f \equiv 1$. Then

$$\|f\|_\infty = \operatorname{ess\,sup}|1| = 1 < \infty,$$

so $f \in L_\infty$. However, for every $p \ge 1$:

$$\|f\|_p = \left(\int_X 1^p\,d\mu\right)^{1/p} = \mu(X)^{1/p} = \infty^{1/p} = \infty.$$

Hence $\sup_{p \ge 1}\|f\|_p = \infty$, and the property fails. $\checkmark$

---

### Conclusion

The condition that must be imposed on $(X, \Sigma, \mu)$ is

$$\boxed{\mu(X) < \infty},$$

i.e., the measure space must be **finite**. This is both necessary and sufficient: finite total measure guarantees $\sup_{p\ge 1}\|f\|_p \le \|f\|_\infty \cdot \max(1,\mu(X)) < \infty$ for every $f \in L_\infty$, while infinite total measure is refuted by the single counterexample $f \equiv 1$.

